welcome to CBS this morning we're going  to begin with major developments on an  overnight job action against one of the  country's big Three automakers for the  first time in more than a decade workers  are staging a nationwide strike against  General Motors Auto Workers leadership  says more than 48 thousand of its  members are part of a work stoppage that  began at midnight the union is demanding  what it calls fair wages affordable  health care and more job security Dean  Reynolds is at GM's Detroit ham track  assembly plant where workers are  protesting this morning even what  prompted this walkout  well the union's contract with GM  expired on Saturday after months of  negotiations and while those talks are  expected to continue later today the  strike effectively shuts down some 50 GM  sites from Texas to Michigan and could  adversely impact operations in Canada  and Mexico Union Auto Workers started  gathering around midnight at this  Detroit General Motors plant and others  like it across the country the strike  comes after GM's 2015 labor deal with  the Union expired Saturday over fault  lines ranging from wages to health care  coverage we stood up for GM when they  needed us you deserve a fair contract  because we have we've helped make this  company what it is almost a decade after  its 2009 bankruptcy General Motors made  8.1 billion dollars last year and its  CEO earned nearly 22 million dollars  according to the company's previous  union contract GM's hourly Auto Workers  make anywhere from roughly forty four to  ninety nine thousand dollars a year now  the Union argues it deserves a bigger  share of the profits it helped create  but GM leadership insists its proposed  contract is competitive we think this  offer is very sound and strong with  investments in job creation the auto  makers suggested deal includes over  seven billion dollars in domestic  investment more than 54  new jobs and a ratification payment of  $8,000 per worker I think there's a lot  of frustration on both sides the union  vice-president Terry did it says GM's  terms represent some progress but not  enough and the stakes are high both  parties will work hard to to reach that  agreement there's thousands of families  depending on that now the walkout is  happening at the same time as a federal  investigation of top union officials  involving allegations of embezzling  stealing and otherwise misusing union  funds for personal benefit GM says it is  deeply concerned by those allegations  but a union official tells us that they  will not hinder contract negotiations  Tony all right Dean thank you very much
 

